3194139536398 17592186045417 true] [17592186096690 1038 #edb/ref 17592186572123 13194139536398 17592186045417 true] [17592186096690 1038 #edb/ref 17592186572148 13194139536398 17592186045417 true] [17592186096700 1038 #edb/ref 17592186572134 13194139536398 17592186045417 true] [17592186096700 1038 #edb/ref 17592186572198 13194139536398 17592186045417 true] [17592186096813 1038 #edb/ref 17592186572109 13194139536398 17592186045417 true] [17592186096813 1038 #edb/ref 17592186572132 13194139536398 17592186045417 true] [17592186096813 1038 #edb/ref 17592186572156 13194139536398 17592186045417 true] [17592186096813 1038 #edb/ref 17592186572196 13194139536398 17592186045417 true] [17592186096814 1038 #edb/ref 17592186572174 13194139536398 17592186045417 true] [17592186096814 1038 #edb/ref 17592186572179 13194139536398 17592186045417 true] [17592186096815 1038 #edb/ref 17592186572119 13194139536398 17592186045417 true] [17592186096815 1038 #edb/ref 17592186572126 13194139536398 17592186045417 true] [17592186096815 1038 #edb/ref 17592186572138 13194139536398 17592186045417 true] [17592186096842 1038 #edb/ref 17592186572150 13194139536398 17592186045417 true] [17592186096843 1038 #edb/ref 17592186572161 13194139536398 17592186045417 true] [17592186096843 1038 #edb/ref 17592186572194 13194139536398 17592186045417 true] [17592186096883 1050 420.0 13194139536398 17592186045417 true] [17592186096884 1050 480.0 13194139536398 17592186045417 true] [17592186097161 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536398 17592186045417 true] [17592186097335 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536398 17592186045417 true] [17592186097832 1064 "Consider the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ shown in the picture below. The vector $2\\mathbf{a}$ is the vector that has the same direction as $\\mathbf{a}$ and a length (magnitude) that is twice the length of $\\mathbf{a}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}2\\mathbf{a} &= 2 \\cdot & \\mathbf{a}\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/01/01c393b2df3d39e4f62faca475a26ea835e1ab88266628509d58e21edd36c79b.png)\n\nOn the other hand, $-1.5\\mathbf{a}$ is the vector that has the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{a}$ and its magnitude is $1.5$ times the magnitude of $\\mathbf{a}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}-1.5\\mathbf{a} &= 1.5 \\cdot & \\mathbf{a}\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/73/73ea353af74ab9518ea7a963985ad26ad36723c7e3a335391628f1eba208139e.png)\n\nNotice that $2\\mathbf{a}$ and $-1.5\\mathbf{a}$ are both parallel to $\\mathbf a$, and are parallel to each other." 13194139536398 17592186045417 false] [17592186097832 1064 "Consider the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ shown in the picture below. The vector $2\\mathbf{a}$ is the vector that has the same direction as $\\mathbf{a}$ and a length (magnitude) that is twice the length of $\\mathbf{a}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n|2\\mathbf{a}|=2 \\cdot |\\mathbf{a}|\n$$\n\n![](images/01/01c393b2df3d39e4f62faca475a26ea835e1ab88266628509d58e21edd36c79b.png)\n\nOn the other hand, $-1.5\\mathbf{a}$ is the vector that has the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{a}$ and its magnitude is $1.5$ times the magnitude of $\\mathbf{a}$: \n\n$$\n|-1.5\\mathbf{a}|=1.5 \\cdot |\\mathbf{a}|\n$$\n\n![](images/73/73ea353af74ab9518ea7a963985ad26ad36723c7e3a335391628f1eba208139e.png)\n\nNotice that $2\\mathbf{a}$ and $-1.5\\mathbf{a}$ are both parallel to $\\mathbf a,$ and are parallel to each other." 13194139536398 17592186045417 true] [17592186097833 1064 "A **unit vector** is a vector whose magnitude is equal to $1$.\n\nGiven any vector $\\mathbf a$, we can create a unit vector $\\mathbf u$ by dividing $\\mathbf a$ by its magnitude $\\mid \\, \\mathbf{a} \\, \\mid$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{a}}{\\mid \\,\\mathbf{a}\\, \\mid}\n$$\n\nFor example, if\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid \\,\\mathbf{a}\\, &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthen the unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{a}}{2} = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\mathbf{a}\n$$\n\n![](images/c7/c78d5b75c1d5928e7b269a4a67229487b3d3324e9815b11c5fd1f47f63437d82.png)" 13194139536398 17592186045417 false] [17592186097833 1064 "A **unit vector** is a vector whose magnitude is equal to $1.$ \n\nGiven any vector $\\mathbf a$, we can create a unit vector $\\mathbf u$ by dividing $\\mathbf a$ by its magnitude $|\\, \\mathbf{a} \\,|,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} = \\dfrac{ \\mathbf{a} }{ |\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,| }\n$$\n\nFor example, if $|\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,|=2,$ then the unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{a}}{2} = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\mathbf{a}.\n$$\n\n![](images/c7/c78d5b75c1d5928e7b269a4a67229487b3d3324e9815b11c5fd1f47f63437d82.png)" 13194139536398 17592186045417 true] [17592186097834 1064 "For any vector $\\mathbf{a}$ the vector $0 \\cdot \\mathbf{a}$ is equal to the zero vector:\n\n$$\n0\\cdot\\mathbf{a}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nThis means that the zero vector $\\mathbf{0}$ is parallel to *any* vector." 13194139536398 17592186045417 false] [17592186097834 1064 "For any vector $\\mathbf{a}$ the vector $0 \\cdot \\mathbf{a}$ is equal to the zero vector: \n\n$$\n0\\cdot\\mathbf{a}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nThis means that the zero vector $\\mathbf{0}$ is parallel to *any* vector." 13194139536398 17592186045417 true] [17592186097852 1064 "In general, two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are called **parallel** (or **collinear**) if there exists a number $\\lambda$ such that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} = \\lambda \\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nSometimes, we'll use a special notation\n$\\mathbf{a} \\parallel \\mathbf{b}$\nto denote that $\\mathbf{a}$ is parallel to $\\mathbf{b}$." 13194139536398 17592186045417 false] [17592186097852 1064 "In general, two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are called **parallel** (or **collinear**) if there exists a number $\\lambda$ such that \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} = \\lambda \\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nSometimes, we'll use a special notation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} \\parallel \\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nto denote that $\\mathbf{a}$ is parallel to $\\mathbf{b}$." 13194139536398 1759218604541